## Why This Is Asked
New angle adds a cooldown constraint to the classic k-transaction DP, testing multi-state reasoning and recovery of the trade sequence.

## Key Concepts
- DP with cooldown using rest/hold/sold states
- Tracking transaction counts and path reconstruction
- Space optimization to O(k)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitWithCooldown(prices, k) {
  const n = prices.length;
  const INF = -1e15;
  const rest = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const hold = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const sold = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  rest[0][0] = 0;
  hold[0][0] = -prices[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      rest[i][t] = Math.max(rest[i - 1][t], sold[i - 1][t]);
      hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i - 1][t], rest[i - 1][t] - prices[i]);
      if (t > 0) sold[i][t] = hold[i - 1][t - 1] + prices[i];
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return Math.max(...rest[n - 1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extract the exact trade days from the DP tables?
- How would the solution change if cooldown length varied to c days?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests partitioning a sequence into exactly k blocks where block value is non-linear in length. It requires a careful DP with reconstruction and shows understanding of prefix sums and state transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Non-linear block value and reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysProduct(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + a[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pick = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j-1][t-1] + (i - j + 1) * (pref[i] - pref[j-1]);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          pick[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue or divide-and-conquer tricks?
- How would you handle allowing at most k blocks and a penalty for unused blocks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a less-traveled DP angle: maximizing a sum of subarray-specific statistics (distinct counts) with exact segmentation. Requires careful state design and backtracking for reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix end i and segment count t
- Subarray distinct counts D(p,i-1)
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k), Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // Precompute distinct counts D[l][r]
  const D = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      seen.add(A[r]);
      D[l][r] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const bt = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = D[0][i - 1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 1; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const cand = dp[p][t - 1] + D[p][i - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; bt[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i = n, t = k;
  const segs = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = bt[i][t];
    segs.push([p, i - 1]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  segs.reverse();
  return { best: dp[n][k], segments: segs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) memory?
- How would you handle at most k segments instead of exactly k?
- Can you adapt to streaming data or reconstruct segments efficiently for large n?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests the ability to formulate a DP solution over prefixes with a partition constraint, combining range-based computations (maximum values) with block length considerations. It requires clear state definition, proper transition logic, and a strategy for reconstructing the solution from computed results.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over array prefixes and block count parameters
- State transitions focusing on the last block contribution
- Efficient handling of range maximum queries during transitions
- Solution reconstruction using parent pointers or backtracking
- Time-space trade-offs and potential RMQ-based optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP framework structure
function partitionArray(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Base cases: single block
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const maxVal = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
    dp[i][1] = maxVal * i;
  }

  // Fill DP table
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        const maxLast = Math.max(...A.slice(j, i));
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + maxLast * (i-j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, blocksLeft = k;
  while (blocksLeft > 0) {
    const start = par[curr][blocksLeft];
    blocks.push([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    blocksLeft--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the range maximum queries to achieve O(1) time per query?
- What memory optimizations can reduce the space complexity from O(nk)?
- How would the solution change if the blocks could be non-contiguous?
- Can you propose a divide-and-conquer optimization to improve the time complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP setup for segment selection with non-adjacency and reconstruction, a common beginner DP pattern with a twist.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index i and blocks t
- Enforce non-adjacency via s-2 reference
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs; possible optimization to O(nk) time and O(n) space per k

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
          const sum = P[i] - P[s - 1];
          const prev = (s >= 2) ? dp[s - 2][t - 1] : (t - 1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
          const cand = sum + prev;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            take[i][t] = s;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using 'take' from (n, bestT)
  return { maxSum: dp[n][0], blocks: [] };
}
```

